WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.466
In Java, System.out.println looks like any other method call.

2
00:00:07.466 --> 00:00:09.497
It returns almost at once.

3
00:00:09.767 --> 00:00:14.728
But no program on your laptop can touch the screen, the disk or the network.

4
00:00:14.728 --> 00:00:15.903
Not even this one.

5
00:00:16.167 --> 00:00:18.416
So what happens inside that one line?

6
00:00:18.416 --> 00:00:22.916
It ends up asking the kernel, the one program that may touch the hardware.

7
00:00:22.916 --> 00:00:25.531
Let's follow that request in, and back out.

8
00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.972
First, a picture from earlier in this lesson: a bank.

9
00:00:28.972 --> 00:00:31.149
Customers can't walk into the vault.

10
00:00:31.149 --> 00:00:37.861
They fill in a slip, push it under the glass, and a teller checks it, does the work and slides back the result.

11
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:38.581
Map it.

12
00:00:38.581 --> 00:00:40.574
Your program is the customer.

13
00:00:40.574 --> 00:00:42.360
The vault is the hardware.

14
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:48.751
The teller is the kernel, working in kernel mode, where the processor runs every instruction.

15
00:00:48.967 --> 00:00:50.481
The glass is user mode.

16
00:00:50.481 --> 00:00:55.351
In user mode, the processor refuses the instructions that talk to devices.

17
00:00:55.351 --> 00:01:00.485
And the slip is a system call: a request with a number that names the service.

18
00:01:00.767 --> 00:01:03.435
So how does the slip get under the glass?

19
00:01:03.435 --> 00:01:06.428
The obvious idea is an ordinary function call.

20
00:01:06.428 --> 00:01:07.665
The kernel is code.

21
00:01:07.665 --> 00:01:10.137
Jump to its write function and run it.

22
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:12.140
But look at what that allows.

23
00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:15.919
The kernel's code checks permissions first, then does the work.

24
00:01:15.919 --> 00:01:22.217
If a program may jump to any address, it can land right past the check, and run the rest with full power.

25
00:01:22.633 --> 00:01:25.378
That's a customer who climbs over the counter.

26
00:01:25.378 --> 00:01:28.899
Banks have a word for that customer, and it isn't customer.

27
00:01:28.899 --> 00:01:31.763
So the program must never choose where it lands.

28
00:01:32.133 --> 00:01:36.072
What we want is one door, and the kernel decides where it leads.

29
00:01:36.072 --> 00:01:41.427
The processor has exactly that: one special instruction that does three things at once.

30
00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:43.282
It switches to kernel mode.

31
00:01:43.282 --> 00:01:45.136
It saves where the program was.

32
00:01:45.136 --> 00:01:49.623
And it jumps to an entry point the kernel picked when the computer started.

33
00:01:49.867 --> 00:01:52.713
The program can't choose where it lands.

34
00:01:52.713 --> 00:01:54.776
This switch is called a trap.

35
00:01:54.776 --> 00:02:00.041
On arm64, the chip family in an Apple Silicon Mac, the instruction is svc.

36
00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:05.309
One door for everything means the slip must say which service, and with what.

37
00:02:05.309 --> 00:02:11.620
Let's shrink it to the smallest request: write 3 bytes, o, k and a newline, to file descriptor 1.

38
00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:17.354
A file descriptor is a small number a program uses to name something it has open.

39
00:02:17.354 --> 00:02:20.788
1 is standard output, usually your terminal window.

40
00:02:21.067 --> 00:02:30.567
The C library's write function, still in user mode, fills in the slip, in registers: the few dozen small storage slots inside the processor.

41
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:33.027
The service number goes in x8.

42
00:02:33.027 --> 00:02:37.480
Like the form number printed on a bank slip, 64 means write.

43
00:02:37.480 --> 00:02:40.226
The three inputs go in x0, x1 and x2.

44
00:02:40.226 --> 00:02:44.160
x1 holds the address of the bytes, called the buffer.

45
00:02:44.433 --> 00:02:45.638
Then it runs svc.

46
00:02:45.638 --> 00:02:51.593
The processor saves its place, switches to kernel mode and lands at the entry point.

47
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:58.505
The kernel reads x8, finds entry 64 in its system call table, and runs the code for write.

48
00:02:58.733 --> 00:03:01.511
Before it copies anything, look at x1 again.

49
00:03:01.511 --> 00:03:04.162
It's an address, and the program wrote it.

50
00:03:04.162 --> 00:03:06.561
What could a sneaky program put there?

51
00:03:06.561 --> 00:03:07.571
Pause and guess.

52
00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.025
The address of the kernel's own memory.

53
00:03:13.025 --> 00:03:15.763
Then the kernel would write out its own secrets.

54
00:03:16.033 --> 00:03:18.236
So the code for write checks first.

55
00:03:18.236 --> 00:03:20.313
Is descriptor 1 open for writing?

56
00:03:20.313 --> 00:03:23.208
Is the buffer really the program's own memory?

57
00:03:23.208 --> 00:03:26.166
An address from user mode could point anywhere.

58
00:03:26.166 --> 00:03:27.928
The kernel checks every one.

59
00:03:28.133 --> 00:03:31.939
Only then does it copy the 3 bytes out to the terminal.

60
00:03:31.939 --> 00:03:34.845
It puts the result in x0: 3 bytes written.

61
00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:38.303
Then it runs eret, short for exception return.

62
00:03:38.303 --> 00:03:43.455
The processor drops back to user mode, at the instruction right after svc.

63
00:03:43.733 --> 00:03:47.284
The C library returns 3, and your program carries on.

64
00:03:47.284 --> 00:03:49.293
It never touched the hardware.

65
00:03:49.667 --> 00:03:51.363
Here the bank picture breaks.

66
00:03:51.363 --> 00:03:55.050
A teller takes minutes, and serves other people while you wait.

67
00:03:55.050 --> 00:03:58.092
There's no queue here, and no pen on a little chain.

68
00:03:58.333 --> 00:04:03.249
The kernel does the work on your program's own processor, and it's quick.

69
00:04:03.249 --> 00:04:11.061
In the lab box, one tiny call, getpid, which asks for the program's own process number, takes about 150 nanoseconds.

70
00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:14.210
A nanosecond is a billionth of a second.

71
00:04:14.210 --> 00:04:15.738
Once, that's nothing.

72
00:04:15.738 --> 00:04:20.685
A million times, it's about 150 milliseconds spent going in and out.

73
00:04:20.967 --> 00:04:26.908
So a program that writes each line of its notes straight to a file pays the trip for every line.

74
00:04:26.908 --> 00:04:30.559
Gather small writes into bigger ones, and cross less often.

75
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:33.476
So what happens inside that one print?

76
00:04:33.476 --> 00:04:42.469
The library fills in a slip in the registers, svc takes it through the one door, and the kernel checks everything before it answers in x0.

77
00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:45.532
One door, one slip, and every address checked.

78
00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.326
One quick check.

79
00:04:47.326 --> 00:04:50.849
Suppose the slip says 63 in x8, instead of 64.

80
00:04:50.849 --> 00:04:53.454
Which service does the kernel run?

81
00:04:53.454 --> 00:04:55.599
Pause and look at the table.

82
00:04:58.800 --> 00:04:59.162
Read.

83
00:04:59.162 --> 00:05:00.393
Entry 63 is read.

84
00:05:00.393 --> 00:05:06.329
The number on the slip picks the service, and the program never picks the address.
